# Obsidian safety

## Gate

Load on every Obsidian turn that might mutate the vault, and whenever chat
might paste note bodies. Read-only inventory and single-note create/edit of
a named path are fine when Obsidian work is authorized — still refuse dumps.

## Read-only default

List/search notes and create/edit a **named** note are allowed when the
vault surface is in play. Do **not**:

- delete notes or folders
- move or rename notes (breaks wikilinks unless updated)
- bulk-rewrite or mass-retag across folders the user did not name
- wipe or rewrite `.obsidian/` app config
- empty the vault trash / permanently purge
- overwrite a note with unrelated content "from scratch" when an in-place
  edit was enough

…unless the user gave an **explicit verb this turn** naming the
object(s) (delete / move / rename / purge / retag-all / …).

## Destructive checklist

When authorized:

1. Confirm the relative path(s) from inventory — never invent targets.
2. Prefer one note or one named folder; refuse vault-wide wipe.
3. On rename/move: update inbound `[[wikilinks]]` in the same change when
   cheap to find; report what was left unresolved.
4. Receipt: old path → new path (or deleted path). No body dump.

## Refuse dumps

Do **not** paste into chat:

- Full private journals, diaries, or therapy notes
- Credentials, API tokens, or recovery phrases found in notes
- Entire vault trees "for context"

Prefer: titles, relative paths, short excerpts (a few lines), and
summaries.

## Bulk rewrite

Mass frontmatter changes, retagging, or find-replace across many notes
need an explicit bulk verb **and** a named scope (folder or search hit
list). Otherwise edit one note at a time.

## `.obsidian/` config

Treat as sensitive app state. Read only when asked. Never rewrite
`workspace.json`, plugin data, or community-plugin configs without an
explicit verb naming the file.

## Report shape

- Relative path(s) + action taken or refused
- Wikilink follow-ups left unresolved (if any)
- Explicit "body not dumped" when the note was private-sensitive
